</section>

<section class="post-section">
  <article class="post">

    <div class="post-meta">
      <span class="post-cat">LPG &amp; CNG</span>
      <span class="post-date">
        <svg width="13" height="13" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3" y="5" width="18" height="16" rx="2" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="3" y1="9" x2="21" y2="9" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="8" y1="3" x2="8" y2="7" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round"/><line x1="16" y1="3" x2="16" y2="7" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
        <time datetime={published}>{publishedLabel}</time>
      </span>
    </div>

    <figure class="post-hero">
      <img
        src={base + "img/latest-1.jpg"}
        alt="LPG and CNG conversion kit components on a workshop bench"
        width="1920" height="1280"
        loading="eager" decoding="async"
      >
    </figure>

    <p class="post-lead">
      Both LPG and CNG will cut what you spend at the pump. They are not, however,
      interchangeable — they suit different cars, different boot-space needs and
      different driving patterns. Here is how the two actually compare, and how to
      tell which one fits the way you drive.
    </p>

    <h2>The short answer</h2>

    <p>
      If you drive long distances every day, rarely need the boot and have a CNG
      station on your regular route, <strong>CNG</strong> usually gives the lowest
      running cost per kilometre. If you want to keep your boot usable, care about
      the car still feeling like it did on petrol, and would rather refuel quickly,
      <strong>LPG</strong> is normally the better fit.
    </p>

    <h2>They are not the same kind of gas</h2>

    <p>
      The names get used almost interchangeably in conversation, but the two fuels
      behave very differently once they are in your car.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>CNG</strong> is compressed natural gas — mostly methane. It stays a gas
      and has to be squeezed to roughly 200 bar to fit a useful amount on board. That
      pressure is why a CNG cylinder is a thick-walled steel vessel: heavy, and shaped
      as a large cylinder that has to sit upright in your boot.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>LPG</strong> is liquefied petroleum gas — a propane and butane mix. It
      turns liquid at only about 8 to 12 bar, so the tank is far lighter and can be
      made in a doughnut shape that drops into the spare-wheel well. Being liquid in
      the tank, LPG also packs more than twice the energy into the same volume as CNG
      does, which is why a smaller LPG tank can still deliver a sensible range.
    </p>

    <h2>Side by side</h2>

    <div class="post-table-wrap">
      <table class="post-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">&nbsp;</th>
            <th scope="col">LPG</th>
            <th scope="col">CNG</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Running cost</th>
            <td>Lower than petrol</td>
            <td>Usually the lowest per kilometre</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Tank pressure</th>
            <td>About 8–12 bar</td>
            <td>About 200 bar</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Boot space</th>
            <td>Doughnut tank can fit the spare-wheel well — boot stays usable</td>
            <td>Large upright cylinder takes most of the boot</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Added weight</th>
            <td>Comparatively light</td>
            <td>Noticeably heavier</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Power vs petrol</th>
            <td>Small drop — close to petrol feel</td>
            <td>Larger drop, most obvious on hills and overtaking</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Refuelling time</th>
            <td>Similar to filling petrol</td>
            <td>Slower fill, and queues are common</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Combustion heat</th>
            <td>Cooler than CNG</td>
            <td>Hotter — valves need closer attention</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">If it leaks</th>
            <td>Heavier than air, settles low</td>
            <td>Lighter than air, rises and disperses</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Boot space decides it for a lot of owners</h2>

    <p>
      This is the part people underestimate until the kit is fitted. A CNG cylinder is
      big, upright and heavy, and in most sedans and hatchbacks it claims the majority
      of the boot permanently. If you carry family luggage, tools, stock for a business
      or a pram, that is a daily cost you pay forever in exchange for the fuel saving.
    </p>

    <p>
      A toroidal LPG tank sits where the spare wheel lives, so the boot floor stays
      flat and usable. You give up the spare wheel instead — something to plan for,
      but for most owners far easier to live with than losing the boot.
    </p>

    <h2>How the car will feel to drive</h2>

    <p>
      Any gas conversion costs a little power, because gas displaces some of the air
      the engine would otherwise draw in. With LPG the difference is small enough that
      most drivers stop noticing it within a week. With CNG the drop is larger and
      shows up where you have least margin — pulling away on an incline, or overtaking
      with a full car.
    </p>

    <p>
      A properly tuned kit matters more here than the choice of fuel. A well set-up
      CNG car drives better than a badly set-up LPG one, which is why the quality of
      the installation and the calibration afterwards is worth more than any spec
      sheet comparison.
    </p>

    <h2>What changes in your maintenance routine</h2>

    <p>
      A converted car is not a petrol car with a cheaper fuel bill. Plan for these:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Spark plugs</strong> wear faster on gas and need replacing more often, with the type and gap the kit specifies — not whatever was in the car before.</li>
      <li><strong>Valve clearances</strong> need periodic checking. Gas does not cool and lubricate the valve seats the way liquid petrol does, and CNG's hotter burn makes this more important on some engines.</li>
      <li><strong>Gas filters</strong> on the supply line are a consumable, like an oil filter.</li>
      <li><strong>Run petrol occasionally.</strong> Letting the petrol system sit unused for months causes its own problems — stale fuel, a tired pump, sticking injectors.</li>
      <li><strong>Cylinder recertification</strong> is a legal requirement at a set interval. Diarise it; an expired cylinder is both unsafe and uninsurable.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Safety, honestly</h2>

    <p>
      Both fuels are safe when the work is done properly — a certified kit, a tested
      cylinder, correctly routed and secured lines, and a leak test afterwards. Both
      are dangerous when it is not. Almost every gas conversion problem traces back to
      the installation rather than to the fuel.
    </p>

    <div class="post-note">
      <p>
        One practical difference worth knowing: LPG is heavier than air, so a leak
        settles into low spaces rather than dispersing. If you routinely park in a
        basement or over a pit, mention it before you convert — it changes what we
        check and how we advise you.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>So which should you choose?</h2>

    <h3>Choose CNG if</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>You cover high daily mileage and want the lowest cost per kilometre.</li>
      <li>There is a CNG station you pass anyway, and the queue does not bother you.</li>
      <li>You rarely need boot space, or the car is mainly used for ride-sharing or commuting alone.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Choose LPG if</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>You need the boot — family car, luggage, business use.</li>
      <li>You want the car to keep feeling close to how it drives on petrol.</li>
      <li>You would rather refuel in a couple of minutes than wait in a line.</li>
      <li>Your mileage is moderate, so convenience matters more than squeezing out the last bit of saving.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Think again if</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>You drive only a few thousand kilometres a year — the saving will take a long time to pay back the conversion.</li>
      <li>Your engine already has untreated problems. Fix those first; gas will not hide them, and it tends to expose weak ignition and worn valves faster.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Still not sure?</h2>

    <p>
      Bring the car in. We will look at your engine, your boot, and how you actually
      use the car, and tell you plainly which conversion makes sense — including when
      the honest answer is that neither is worth it yet. We will also quote you the
      current price for your specific model rather than a generic figure.
    </p>

    <div class="post-cta">
      <h3>Talk to a conversion specialist</h3>
      <p>Straight advice on LPG and CNG for your car, from the technicians who fit them.</p>
      <div class="post-cta-actions">
        <a href="tel:+8801854226757" class="cta-call">
          <svg class="icon" width="15" height="15" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#icon-phone"/></svg>
          Call 01854226757
        </a>
        <a href="https://wa.me/8801854226757" class="cta-wa" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          <svg class="icon" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#icon-whatsapp"/></svg>
          WhatsApp Us
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <a href="blog.html" class="post-back">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M19 12H5M11 18l-6-6 6-6" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
      Back to Blog
    </a>

  </article>
</section>

</Layout>
